Soft Braille Keyboard
=====================

This repository contains a modernized build of the historical Soft Braille
Keyboard Android IME. The project now builds with Gradle and bundles the
BrailleBack translation pieces it depends on instead of requiring a separate
checkout.

Status
------

* `targetSdk`: Android 16 / API 36
* `compileSdk`: API 36
* `minSdk`: Android 5.0 / API 21
* Expected to run on Android 16 and newer releases, including Android 17
  compatibility mode, as long as the IME APIs remain stable.
* Includes a bundled braille translation backend and a bundled braille display
  backend based on historical BrailleBack components.

Build Requirements
------------------

* Android SDK with platform `android-36`
* Android NDK `27.1.12297006`
* Java 17

Build
-----

Run:

```
export JAVA_HOME=/path/to/jdk-17
export ANDROID_SDK_ROOT=/path/to/Android/Sdk
./gradlew :app:assembleDebug
```

The debug APK is generated at:

```
app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk
```

A convenience copy is also kept at the repository root after local builds:

```
SoftBrailleKeyboard.apk
```

Notes
-----

* The app still contains legacy Java UI code, but the build system and
  manifest were updated for current Android requirements.
* Braille translation uses a bundled subset of BrailleBack plus liblouis
  tables packaged in `app/src/main/res/raw/translationtables.zip`.
* Braille display support now includes:
  `DisplayService`, `DisplayClient`, bundled `brltty` native drivers and
  key tables in `app/src/main/res/raw/keytables.zip`.
* Android 12+ Bluetooth support requires the runtime permission
  `BLUETOOTH_CONNECT`.
* The app now includes a bundled offline quick start guide and direct entry
  points to braille diagnostics and Android accessibility settings from the
  main screen and settings UI.
* Some historical source files for the old Ant/Eclipse build were intentionally
  left in the repository for reference, but they are no longer used by the
  Gradle build.

Braille Display Scope
---------------------

The repository now contains the backend needed to detect and connect supported
braille displays over Bluetooth and USB. This is not yet the same thing as a full
system-wide braille accessibility stack like the original BrailleBack plus
TalkBack combination.

Included now:

* device detection and connection service,
* BRLTTY driver layer,
* key name mappings and key tables,
* client AIDL/API for apps in the same package,
* a bundled accessibility service that mirrors the currently focused UI text to
  the braille display,
* command handling for pan, routing, focus navigation, scrolling, web/list/
  control navigation, semantic section/control/list fallback in native Android
  UI, global actions and basic editable text input,
* USB host enumeration and permission plumbing via the BRLTTY Android USB
  helper, plus BRLTTY USB selectors tuned with VID/PID for known models,
* a diagnostics screen with command testing, USB permission actions,
  per-device profile controls and per-device binding-aware remap,
* native raw key tap (`group/number/press`) exposed through the display event
  pipeline for diagnostics,
* native raw remap now supports argument-aware targets such as route/long-route
  and preserves or fixes arguments on the BRLTTY side before the command reaches
  Java,
* a first true pre-recognition chord overlay now holds candidate raw presses,
  consumes matched raw chords before standard BRLTTY translation and falls back
  to normal BRLTTY processing for non-matching input,
* the diagnostics screen can export the current report and raw key trace to a
  text file in the app diagnostics directory.

Not included yet:

* verified hardware test coverage on current physical devices,
* verified coverage for the new pre-recognition rewrite path on current
  physical devices,
* a deeper per-key press/release rewrite engine for cases where full chord
  interception still is not enough.
